          "tool_use_id": "toolu_0196F2pNjPyhACAp8NrCDapx",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/autoscaling.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Autoscaling\\ndescription: Automatically adjusting the number of running replicas of a service based on observed load.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Autoscaling\\n  - concept/Kubernetes\\n  - tech/HPA\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two independent sources; covers core mechanism and stabilization behavior.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nAutoscaling adjusts replica count for a service automatically instead of requiring a\\nhuman to resize it manually. The controller observes some signal \\u2014 CPU/memory\\nutilization, queue depth, or a custom metric \\u2014 and computes a desired replica count on\\nan interval.\\n\\n## Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)\\n\\nThe Kubernetes HPA controller:\\n- Polls the metrics API at regular intervals\\n- Compares observed metrics (CPU, memory, or custom metrics) against a target threshold\\n- Computes a desired replica count\\n- Uses a **stabilization window** to avoid flapping \\u2014 rapid oscillation between scaling\\n  up and down in response to small, transient metric fluctuations\\n\\nHPA operates at the pod level only. It does not provision new cluster nodes; that is\\nthe responsibility of the separate **Cluster Autoscaler** controller.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler documentation and behavior (eval://steering-adoption/sample-02)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
